  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled conditions return this afternoon and \nevening with scattered showers and thunderstorms developing. \n\nAfternoon analysis shows weak low pressure has developed along a \nslow-moving/stalled cold front. The front is noted on surface \nobservations, currently stretching from near the I-85 corridor \nnortheast into the central Ches Bay and MD Eastern Shore. Winds are \nmainly N or NE behind the boundary and S or SW ahead of the front. \nNot much more than a wind shift is noted with the front as temps and \ndew points are similar across the boundary. The developing weak \nsurface low will tend to further slow the front's progress across \nthe region. Showers and storms have started to develop both ahead of \nand behind the front this afternoon. Mesoanalysis shows moderate \nMLCAPE values 1000-2000 J/kg over much of the area with deep layer \nshear 25-35 kt (35-40 kt for MD portions of the Eastern Shore). SPC \nhas most of the area in a Marginal Risk (1/5) for strong storms this \nafternoon and evening. The primary severe threat will come from \nstrong straight line winds in wet downbursts.\n\nMoisture is a bit more limited with most of the 2+\" PWATs currently \nconfined to NE NC and offshore. Deeper moisture may spread back to \nthe north and west for areas ahead of the weak surface low late this \nafternoon and evening. WPC has the southeastern portion of the area \n(including much of Hampton Roads) highlighted in a Day 1 Slight ERO \n(2/5) with the remainder of the area in a Marginal ERO (1/5). \nIsolated to scattered instance of flash flooding are possible, \nespecially across urban or poor drainage areas. Rain chances linger \ninto the first part of the night before generally dry conditions \nreturn after midnight into early Saturday morning.\n\nThe front weakens but will continue to linger across far SE VA into \nNE NC on Saturday. Scattered showers and storms will continue across \nour southern areas Saturday afternoon, with the highest coverage \nclosest to the Albemarle Sound. WPC has locations south of the VA/NC \nborder highlighted in a Day 2 Marginal ERO for Saturday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions prevail during the early to middle \nportion of next week.\n\nAn expansive 500mb ridge (1.5 to 2 standard deviations above normal) \nbuilds from the Central Plains into the Mid Atlantic and Southeast \nearly next week. Both NBM and deterministic guidance continue to \nfavor strong diabatic heating Monday through Thursday with highs in \nthe low to mid 90s for inland areas. 850mb temperatures of 19-21C \nand strong 1000-850mb thickness schemes support deep mixing during \npeak heating, which should pull afternoon dew points down into the \nupper 60s to around 70. Peak heat indices will likely stay below \n105, generally hovering around 100-104 each day. Rain chances remain \nminimal through Wednesday with increasing rain chances later in the \nweek as another front approaches.",
